Far from its source, a small portion of an EM wave behaves as a **plane wave**: the fields have the same value everywhere on any plane perpendicular to the direction of travel. For a plane wave in free space:

- the electric field $\vec E$ is perpendicular to the direction of propagation;
- the magnetic field $\vec B$ is perpendicular to the direction of propagation;
- $\vec E$ and $\vec B$ are perpendicular to each other.

Neither field has any component along the direction of travel. A wave in which the oscillating quantity is perpendicular to the direction of propagation is called **transverse**, so EM waves are transverse waves. The figure shows a wave travelling along $x$ with $\vec E$ along $y$ and $\vec B$ along $z$.
